176,\n      \"score\": 0.285,\n      \"title\": \"Prevent context manager to overwrite previous one in jupyter\",\n      \"state\": \"closed\",\n      \"state_reason\": \"completed\",\n      \"strong\": false,\n      \"excerpt\": \"When in Jupyter lab, if I call independent instances of a `Progress()`, for example by mapping a function that invokes a `Progress()` through an iterable, the context manager overwrites/deletes previous instances along the iteration. For example (inside a Jupyter Lab notebook) inspired from code in the tutorial: ``` import time from rich.progress import Progress def run_snippet(iteration): with Progress() as progress: task1 = progress.add_task(\\\"[red]Downloading...\\\", total=5) while not progress.finished: progress.update(task1, advance=0.5) #progress.log(f'this iteration {iteration}') <- uncomme ...\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"number\": 3701,\n      \"score\": 0.281,\n      \"title\": \"[BUG] Behavior of console.print() with empty *args is different from regular print()\",\n      \"state\": \"open\",\n      \"state_reason\": null,\n      \"strong\": false,\n      \"excerpt\": \"**Describe the bug** Expected that `console.print(end=\\\"!\\\")` will print only `!` as regular `python print()`, but it print line break without ending symbol. ```python from rich.console import Console console = Console() for f in ( lambda: print(\\\"A\\\", end=\\\"!\\\"), lambda: print(end=\\\"!\\\"), lambda: console.print(\\\"A\\\", end=\\\"!\\\"), lambda: console.print(end=\\\"!\\\"), ): print(\\\"=>\\\", end=\\\"\\\") f() print(\\\"<=\\\") ``` Output: ``` \\u276f python3 test.py =>A!<= =>!<= =>A!<= => <= ``` rich == 14.0.0\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"number\": 3284,\n      \"score\": 0.221,\n      \"title\": \"Is there a way to print the line number while console.log'ging a single line?\",\n      \"state\": \"closed\",\n      \"state_reason\": \"completed\",\n      \"strong\": false,\n      \"excerpt\": \"I was recommended rich's `console.log()` as a way to 